.TH AWSV4 2
.SH NAME
awsv4: sign, signature, Creds, Request \- AWS Signature Version 4
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "sys.m";
include "keyring.m";
include "awsv4.m";
awsv4 := load AWSV4 AWSV4->PATH;
Creds, Request: import awsv4;

init:       fn(): string;
sign:       fn(c: ref Creds, req: ref Request, amzdate: string): list of (string, string);
signature:  fn(c: ref Creds, req: ref Request, amzdate: string): (string, string);
amzdatenow: fn(): string;
sha256hex:  fn(b: array of byte): string;

Creds: adt {
    access: string;  secret: string;  token: string;
    region: string;  service: string;
};
Request: adt {
    method: string;  host: string;  path: string;  query: string;
    headers: list of (string, string);
    payload: array of byte;
};
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Awsv4
computes AWS Signature Version 4 request signatures \(em the scheme
every current AWS API authenticates with. It is pure computation with
no network of its own; paired with
.IR https (2)
it makes signed AWS calls entirely within this tree, needing no host
tooling. It is built on the HMAC-SHA256 and SHA-256 primitives
.IR keyring (2)
already provides.
.PP
.B Init
must be called once. A
.B Creds
holds the access key id, secret access key, an optional STS session
.B token
(nil if none), and the target
.B region
and
.B service
(e.g.
.BR us-east-1 ,
.BR s3 ).
A
.B Request
describes the call: HTTP
.BR method ,
bare
.B host
(no scheme),
.B path
(already percent-encoded where required;
.B /
if empty), raw
.B query
string (\c
.BR "a=1&b=2" ,
already encoded, any order \(em
.B sign
canonicalizes and sorts it), extra
.B headers
to include in the signature, and the request
.B payload
(nil for an empty body).
.PP
.B Sign
returns the full list of
.RI ( name ,\  value )
headers to send: X-Amz-Date, X-Amz-Content-Sha256 (for
.B s3
only \(em it is that service's own required header), optionally
X-Amz-Security-Token, the caller's own headers, and the Authorization
header carrying the signature. It deliberately does
.I not
return a Host header:
.B host
is part of the signature, but
.IR https (2)
supplies its own Host from the URL (an identical value), and sending
two makes AWS reject the request. Hand the returned list straight to
.IR https (2)'s
.BR request .
.PP
.I Amzdate
is an ISO8601 basic UTC timestamp,
.BR 20150830T123600Z .
Passing it explicitly keeps signing deterministic and testable;
.B amzdatenow
builds one from the clock for live use.
.B Signature
returns just the hex signature and the string-to-sign, for checking
against published vectors.
.B Sha256hex
is the lowercase-hex SHA-256 used for the payload and canonical-request
hashes.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/awsv4.b
.br
.B /module/awsv4.m
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR awsv4test (1),
.IR s3 (1),
.IR https (2),
.IR keyring (2)
.SH BUGS
Only the SigV4 header-authorization scheme is implemented \(em not
query-string (presigned-URL) signing, not the streaming
.B STREAMING-AWS4-HMAC-SHA256-PAYLOAD
chunked body encoding, and not SigV4A (multi-region). The whole
payload is hashed in memory, so it is unsuited to very large bodies.
Canonical-header handling emits each header once and does not merge
duplicate header names (no AWS API this targets sends them). Verified
against AWS's own aws-sig-v4-test-suite
.B get-vanilla
vector and cross-checked against
.B botocore
(both agree byte for byte); see
.IR awsv4test (1).
